we were talking about the way that the  labour party is trying to navigate its  way on the path back to power in a  landscape with echoes of the 1970s and  the 1980s  delegates could do worse than listen to  some of the parties elder states people  including our next guest charles clark  was a labour councillor worked as chief  of staff to neil kinnick in opposition  became an mp himself and served as  education secretary and home secretary  under tony blair  you could say he's seen it and done it  all  and he joins us now  charles clark  how do you think they're doing so far  this weekend well first of all trevor  good morning nice to see you in the  fresh sunday morning  i think they're doing okay i think  politics has been exceptionally  difficult in the time of covid this is  the first really large person-to-person  gathering that's taken place  there are many many issues which need to  be resolved uh on some of them you've  covered in this program today  but i think they've put themselves up as  a realistic alternative to the  conservatives and that's what they need  to be at this stage they need to take it  further now to really set out what the  alternatives are there's more work to do  well i want to come to that but if i may  first  deal with the the issue of the moment  you  were have been you were a minister for a  long time this weekend the country faces  a petrol crisis prospectus shortages in  the supermarket um  you  actually faced a rather more challenging  christ set of crises at home at home  secretary the fuel duty crisis foot and  and when you were party chairman so on  um what would you be advising the  cabinet to be doing right now  get real look at the absolute situation  uh in a sense grant shap's got off a bit  light he's the puppet and the puppet  master the organ grinder is pretty patel  the home secretary the whole issue here  is about the immigration criteria and  under what basis you can give people the  right to work in this country brexit  made the decision clear it wasn't the eu  it was the british government so the  question is could this have been  predicted well yes it could you gave  earlier today the example of uh branch  chaps himself predicting it in august so  if it could be predicted why didn't the  steps weren't steps in place earlier  secondly why is it so few we're talking  about 4 000 or so  road drivers when there are 100 000  drivers short when we've got 13 000  drivers less than when we were last in  the eu from the eu and then finally why  only these sectors why only uh  road drivers and uh poultry what about  care workers you've been talking to  christina just now there's significant  shortages across the economy the  government has it in its power to  resolve those questions right now and  pretty patellars held out to say no  change no change no change no change  until yesterday morning when the prime  minister overruled her and said they put  in the scheme that they're announcing  this morning too little too late you've  got to face up to the issue predict it  get on the case beforehand and then pull  out all the stops at the moment the  government isn't pulling out any stops  at all it's it it's various injunctions  as to how people should behave but the  reality is when we had those issues in  autumn 2000 on the field drivers dispute  the foot and mouth crisis we pulled out  all the stops at that time whatever  problem was needed including up to  including using the army if necessary to  help move things forward i don't see any  evidence that grant traps himself or his  organ grinder priti patel are taking  these issues at all seriously do you  think that's the core point do you think  pretty patel is up to it of course she  isn't she's been a hopeless home  secretary and she should have been sat  the last time around all the  commentators just a couple of weeks ago  all the commentators rightly say that  she's got some grip on boris johnson  he's frightened of her in the back  benches or whatever it might be which  means she's unstackable as far as boris  johnson's concerned but her track record  is appalling it was appalling through  covid uh in the whole way she dealt with  policing the civil liberties issued  through kovid it's appalling on  immigration crossing the  channel it's appalling on this issue now  she is the person who has the  responsibility for the quotas of people  allowed to get visas in this country and  on what basis it is she's deciding that  she's been overruled by the prime  minister for this short announcement  we've got this morning uh but she is the  person who's responsible and people like  grant chaps aren't pulling the true  calling the tricks at all they're simply  uh doing what she said let's come back  to labor you and neil kinnick's chief of  staff  at the time when  the now very famous speech uh denouncing  militant uh  took took place or delivered that that  speech and you in fact had been  in liverpool trying to  deal with the problem as you saw it of  the far left the militant and so on now  it's pretty clear that the party  leadership wants to try to tackle  a similar problem now if you were neil  kinnick's chief of staff now what would  you be a neil king a big partner  kirsten's chief of staff now what would  you be advising him to do firstly you  have to prepare in each case in each  area it's policy it's organization it's  communication you have to prepare that's  what we did on liverpool for that speech  about militant that that you referred to  with neil that applies to the  constitutional changes we're talking  about i'm in favor of what's going to go  through today i hope because i think  it's a step in the right direction i  think here's done well to put it on the  agenda but you've got to prepare very  fully and very comprehensive you can't  just bounce it on everybody  secondly the outstanding issues that  still remain a large number of  disciplinary cases in the labour party  about anti-semitism and so on thousands  i gather still unresolved i would say  those have to be resolved and sorted out  categorically they can't hang about in  all of these issues as they have to move  forward that's on the organizational  side on the policy side you have to  develop in each area of policy in an  alternative of what we would be doing we  labor would be doing were we in  government now so it's no good simply  attacking opposing by attacking the  government and saying you're a load of  rubbish they are a load of rubbish but  that's not enough you've got to say if  we were there we'd be doing this in a  different way whether it's the migration  dispute whether it was what we're  talking about today the fuel drivers  dispute or whatever on every area we  have to say to the british people there  is a better way of doing this uh dealing  with this problem and we would be doing  it in this way on cop26 when it comes  around on security the foreign policy  issues whatever it is and that has to  emerge  weakly as it comes through it's not a  great policy for the future it's a sense  of what the labor alternative is we get  a sense of that i get the sense that um  you don't think that actually that's  what we're getting this weekend instead  what we are probably going to get is 24  hours of conversation about  the word scum now you've just used the  word rubbish about government  do you think that that kind of language  helps in any way  in general no and i try and censor  myself sometimes sometimes  unsuccessfully on those kinds of things  but i i don't think that's the right way  to proceed but i think it is fair for an  opposition to say is the government  competent in dealing with this question  so in the case of what we're just  talking about now is the government  competent in dealing with the migration  issue with the fuel dispute issue or  whatever if it's not competent we should  draw attention to it but this is a but  this is uh i'm really really  asking a question about the way that  politics is projected  um this is going to hang over the labour  leadership for some time isn't it  is the deputy leader a person that you  can trust to put out there  she certainly is i i think it may hang  over it depends what people like you and  how people like you talk about it i  wasn't aware about till i heard it you  mentioned on this program today it's not  an enormous issue  as i'm sure andrew herself would say  today it's not the right way to conduct  yourself in these circumstances and  she'd seek to  control herself in a way that ensured  that bad language of that kind or my use  of the word rubbish is not used uh  because that's a bad way of conducting  politics but i don't think it's the  central question the central question is  what are the policies government  actually doing and what should labour be  offering as an alternative charles clark  thank you very much thank you

